  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1207 AM CDT Sun Apr 19 2026\n\nCool and unsettled weather is expected to continue for Monday as a \nshortwave trough will set up over northwest Mexico.  This, combined \nwith gulf moisture making a northwestward return along the Rio \nGrande through west Texas, should result in widespread rainfall. \nModels show PWAT values over an inch across the Concho Valley and \nEdwards Plateau which could result in some locally heavy rainfall \namounts on Monday. That being said, models tend to struggle with \nscenarios like this when there is a lack of good forcing mechanisms \nto provide a focus for heavy precipitation. Extensive cloud cover \nand rain could also result in rather cool high temperatures around \n60 that day, depending on how the precipitation shield sets up.  The \nupper-level wave should continue moving eastward across Texas on \nTuesday. Rain chances should gradually come to an end and allow for \nwarmer temperatures with better return flow and sunshine.\n\nFor the second half of the upcoming week, the upper-level flow \npattern looks to shift again. A broad upper-level low will move out \nof the Pacific and into the western US on Wednesday and into the \nnorthern Plains on Thursday.  For now, models show several waves \nrotating around the base of this trough on Thursday and Friday. This \nsetup would push a series of systems through the southern Plains on \nThursday through Saturday.  These systems would interact with warm, \nmoist southerly flow each day, and could result in isolated to\nwidely scattered thunderstorms along the dryline each afternoon \nand evening. For now, rain chances were kept low due to the\nuncertainty of how these systems will progress and low coverage of\nprecipitation.",
